/*
  VaultDeniability — privacy pair B green: a cold database dump does
  not reveal which stores are secret vaults (crib graph://1e005d5c-c11
  §24). Bead auto-u8pcx (spike: auto-pdvu6). Tracker 8277c76c.

  Sealed stores pool in one shared settings set with uniform opaque
  row keys and payload shapes: a secret vault's dump row is
  <h(<pepper, name>), senc(payload, key)> — the address derived from
  the sealed pepper, the payload sealed. §24's claim: a dump
  CONTAINING a secret vault is indistinguishable from one without, as
  long as the pepper stays sealed (row COUNT is stated as revealed and
  is out of scope — the bi-system compares same-shaped rows).

  Run with `tamarin-prover --diff --prove`. LEFT of the diff: the row
  is a real secret vault. RIGHT: the same-shaped row is pure noise (no
  vault exists). Equivalence = deniability.

  Calibration: VaultDeniabilityPlainAddr.spthy — deterministic
  plaintext row addresses. Equivalence MUST be falsified there.

  Abstraction: one row stands for the dump (multi-row dumps and
  pepper-compromise variants are future increments); h and senc are
  the builtin symbolic primitives.
*/

theory VaultDeniability
begin

builtins: symmetric-encryption, hashing

rule Dump_Row:
  [ Fr(~pepper), Fr(~data), Fr(~k), Fr(~r1), Fr(~r2) ]
  -->
  [ Out(diff( <h(<~pepper, 'vault'>), senc(~data, ~k)>
            , <~r1, ~r2> ))
  ]

end
